---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
const components = `from power_pptx import BBox
from power_pptx.design import Card, Column, MetricTile, Row, TextBlock
from power_pptx.util import Inches, Pt

Column([
    TextBlock("A calmer operating rhythm.", font="DejaVu Sans",
              size_pt=34, min_size_pt=30, max_lines=1, bold=True),
    Row([
        MetricTile("94%", "Adoption", font="DejaVu Sans"),
        Card([TextBlock("Make the next move clear.", font="DejaVu Sans")]),
    ], gap=Pt(18), height=Inches(3)),
], gap=Pt(24)).render(slide, BBox.from_inches(.65, .6, 12, 6), strict_fonts=True)`;
const fonts = `from power_pptx.design import Column, DataTable, TextBlock
from power_pptx.text import register_font_dir

register_font_dir("assets/fonts")   # or POWER_PPTX_FONT_DIRS=assets/fonts

Column([
    TextBlock("Active customers", font="Inter", size_pt=9, min_size_pt=9,
              case="upper", letter_spacing=Pt(1.4), weight="semibold"),
    DataTable(rows, columns=[1, 1, 2], font="Inter", max_lines=1, truncate=True),
], fill_last=True)`;
const hybrid = `from power_pptx.compose import from_html
prs = from_html("brief.html", mode="hybrid", reference_dir="browser-reference")`;
const review = `pip install 'power-pptx[embedding,review]'
python -m playwright install chromium
# Install LibreOffice separately; use Poppler or the PDFium review extra.
power-pptx review deck.pptx --out-dir review
power-pptx review brief.html --mode hybrid --out-dir review
power-pptx review one.pptx two.pptx --out-dir batch
power-pptx review deck.pptx --font-dir assets/fonts --out-dir review`;
---
<DocsLayout title="Modern Authoring Workflows"
  description="Measured native components, annotated HTML, constrained typography, browser figures, and installed visual review.">
  <p class="lead">Carry a strong web design into editable slides with measured components,
    explicit HTML annotations, and a review bundle for every page.</p>
  <h2 id="components">Measured native components</h2>
  <Code code={components} lang="python" />
  <p>Rows distribute flexible width; columns measure natural heights and distribute spare height
    using grow weights. Padding and gaps use Pt/Inches lengths. Font sizes use integer points.
    Badges have intrinsic width. Trees are checked before shapes are created; impossible layouts
    raise LayoutOverflowError. Text and shapes stay editable, with measured line breaks saved.</p>
  <p>Use NativeContent(builder, height=...) to reserve a chart or table slot. It receives
    (slide, bbox); the builder’s own behavior is outside layout preflight. Give components unique
    names to retrieve their first shape from the result. Container overlap declarations preserve
    sibling collision checks.</p>
  <h2 id="fonts">Bundled fonts, tracked labels, and tables</h2>
  <Code code={fonts} lang="python" />
  <p>register_font_dir makes fonts shipped with your project measurable without installing them,
    so strict_fonts=True behaves the same on a laptop, in Docker, and in CI. Each style is looked
    up separately; register_font_family pins explicit files per style and weight. TextBlock
    measures letter_spacing, weight, and case, so a tracked label that no longer fits raises
    LayoutOverflowError. Medium and SemiBold faces are written by their legacy family name, which is
    how PowerPoint selects them.</p>
  <p>A column stretched by its row leaves spare height under its last card. Preflight warns with
    LayoutWarning; Column(fill_last=True) or grow on the last child fixes it, and slide.lint()
    reports the same problem as RaggedEdge. DataTable measures row heights from cell text and can
    truncate cells with an ellipsis. Set SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH for byte-identical builds.</p>
  <h2 id="typography">Typography constraints</h2>
  <p>TextFrame.fit_text accepts min_size, max_lines, preserve_breaks, and line_spacing.
    preserve_breaks forbids additional wrapping; an impossible fit leaves formatting unchanged.
    power_pptx.text.measure_text provides read-only line and size measurements. Use installed
    fonts and strict metrics for final output; metric files do not embed fonts.</p>
  <h2 id="hybrid">Annotated HTML to editable slides</h2>
  <Code code={hybrid} lang="python" />
  <table><thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>data-pptx="text"</td><td>Editable rich text with browser-measured line breaks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>data-pptx="chart"</td><td>Native chart and workbook from data-pptx-chart JSON</td></tr>
    <tr><td>data-pptx="table"</td><td>Editable table cells, merges, basic fills and borders</td></tr>
    <tr><td>data-pptx="capture"</td><td>Independent captured visual</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Unmarked CSS stays in the background image. Annotated descendants are removed from the base
    and become foreground layers ordered by z-index and DOM order. Annotations cannot nest,
    overflow the section, or depend on arbitrary CSS stacking. Hybrid requires contain fitting
    and installed native fonts; font_map supports deliberate substitutions.</p>
  <p>Native text supports basic rich runs, color, opacity, alignment, and padding. Put backgrounds
    and decoration on unmarked parents. Transforms, mixed sizes, RTL/vertical text, text-transform,
    letter spacing, and shadow/gradient text need separate text annotations or capture. Tables use
    plain cell text. Charts use a minimal native style and explicit data; supported types are
    column, bar, line, area, pie, and doughnut. Browser chart styling is not translated.</p>
  <h2 id="figures">Browser figure export</h2>
  <p>add_plotly_figure(..., backend="browser", format="svg") uses self-contained Plotly.js and
    embeds SVG plus a browser PNG fallback. Install Plotly and the HTML extra with Chromium.
    backend="auto" tries Kaleido then the browser; backend="kaleido" disables fallback.
    figure_slide forwards options through plotly_options. Matplotlib keeps its direct exporter.</p>
  <h2 id="review">Review every saved slide</h2>
  <Code code={review} lang="bash" />
  <p>The installed command accepts PPTX, HTML, JSON, and YAML. It saves PPTX, PDF, all PNGs,
    contact sheets, bundled schema/package checks, round-trip checks, font availability, an audit,
    and a local gallery. HTML inputs retain original browser references. --reference-html or
    --reference-dir enables comparisons; --max-error makes pixel differences a gate.
    --font-policy error gates missing explicit native fonts. Theme fonts are outside that inventory.
    --font-dir registers a font folder and hands it to LibreOffice, so previews use the real face
    instead of a fallback.</p>
  <p>Inspect every rendered page for wrapping, clipping, contrast, and composition, then record
    findings in REVIEW.md. Automated reports mark visual review pending. Structural checks and
    pixel differences do not certify appearance or Microsoft PowerPoint playback.</p>
  <p>The source examples/authoring_workflows/ directory builds three decks and nine slides using
    these public APIs. The bundled skill’s authoring-workflows.md documents the full contract.</p>
</DocsLayout>
